kosha column

  -- a vertical structural element carrying compressive load.
  -- the column is the most fundamental element of the frame structural system.
  -- load path: floor → beam → column → foundation → ground.
  -- the column does one thing: carry the load downward.
  --
  -- buckling: the critical failure mode of columns (not crushing).
  --   a long slender column fails by bowing sideways before the material crushes.
  --   Euler's formula: Pcr = π²EI / (KL)²
  --   Pcr: critical buckling load. E: Young's modulus. I: second moment of area.
  --   K: effective length factor (end conditions). L: column length.
  --   slenderness ratio: KL/r (r = radius of gyration). high = buckling risk.
  --   a fat short column: crushes (compression failure). tall thin column: buckles.
  --
  -- classical column orders (Greek and Roman):
  --   Doric:      simplest. no base. plain circular capital. Parthenon.
  --   Ionic:      scroll (volute) capital. more slender.
  --   Corinthian: most ornate. acanthus leaf capital. tallest.
  --   Tuscan:     Roman simplification of Doric. no fluting.
  --   Composite:  Roman combination of Ionic + Corinthian.
  --   the order determines proportions: column height = N × diameter (N varies by order).
  --
  -- column proportions encode structural logic:
  --   Doric: 6-8 diameters tall (stubby, strong impression).
  --   Corinthian: 9-10 diameters (slender, elegant).
  --   the proportion communicates structural confidence.

